<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bay of Kotor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Boka Kotorska</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bay of Kotor is a winding, fjord-like bay on the Adriatic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, surrounded by steep limestone mountains. Its towns, such as Kotor and Perast, were held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1420 to 1797 and were famous for seafaring. It later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> and the Austrian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, and was an important naval base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor.jpg" alt="Bay of Kotor" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kotor_aerial_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The bay is about 28 kilometres long, with a shoreline of 107.3 kilometres, and is closed in by two massifs of the Dinaric Alps, the Orjen mountains to the west and the Lovćen mountains to the east. It is a ria, the drowned valley of the vanished Bokelj River, and consists of several broad bays joined by narrower channels: the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a> at the main entrance, then the Bay of Tivat, and beyond the Verige Strait, which is only 340 metres wide at its narrowest, the inner bays of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/risan">Risan</a> and Kotor. The steep mountains make it one of the wettest places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and cold Bora winds blow down from them in winter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The bay has been inhabited since antiquity, and South Slavs settled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> from the 6th century. In 1171 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a> sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and launched an offensive towards Kotor, after which the bay came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a>. In 1219 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a> founded the seat of the Eparchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a> on Prevlaka, and Kotor became a major centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a>. After 1371 local lords and the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> contended for the region, and Kotor was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> from 1391 until it accepted Venetian rule in 1420.</p><p>Venetian rule lasted until 1797. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Novi in 1482 and held the northern parts of the bay until the end of the 17th century, when the whole area became Venetian. In 1654 the people of Perast defended their town against an Ottoman attack. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> gave the bay to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rule in 1797; French rule followed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaties of Tilsit</a> in 1807, as part of the Napoleonic Kingdom of Italy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>. A union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> was declared in 1813, but in 1815 the bay was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and placed in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. The Serbian army entered it on 7 November 1918, and since 1945 it has been part of Montenegro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The well-preserved medieval towns of Kotor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/risan">Risan</a>, Tivat, Perast, Prčanj and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herceg-novi">Herceg Novi</a>, with their surroundings, were designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979. The bay has a long maritime tradition: Kotor had a naval academy, the Scuola Nautica, and the fleet of the Boka peaked at 300 ships in the 18th century. Its people, the Bokelj, are a South Slavic community, most of them Orthodox Christians and some Catholics, and its many Orthodox and Catholic churches and monasteries draw pilgrims and visitors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bay of Kotor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He joined them with Dalmatia, Ragusa and the <b>Bay of Kotor</b>, already in French hands, to form the Illyrian Provinces, cutting Austria off from the sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Congress of Vienna it recovered the coast, now including the former Republic of Ragusa and the <b>Bay of Kotor</b>, and organised it in 1815 as the Kingdom of Dalmatia, a crown land of the Austrian Empire governed from Zara.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dalmatia">Kingdom of Dalmatia</a></li><li><blockquote>Stefan Crnojević, ruling from around 1451, allied with Venice and joined Skanderbeg's League of Lezhë, holding the territory around Lake Skadar and the highlands behind the <b>Bay of Kotor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/zeta-under-the-crnojevici">Zeta under the Crnojevići</a></li></ul></section>
